#%PAM-1.0
# =============================================================================
# aquarius-lock — how the lock screen is allowed to ask "is this really you"
# =============================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
#
# When you press Super+L and then type your password, the Aquarius Shell does
# NOT check that password. It is not allowed to, and the reason is worth saying
# plainly: a program that could check a password by itself could also be
# tricked into saying yes.
#
# Instead it asks PAM — the part of Linux whose whole job is answering "is this
# really you". This file is the list of rules PAM follows when the lock screen
# asks. It is named `aquarius-lock` because that is the name the shell asks for,
# in lock/LockState.qml:
#
#     PamContext { config: "aquarius-lock" }
#
# If this file were missing, the lock screen would refuse to start a check and
# would say so on screen. That is on purpose too: a lock screen that silently
# ignores your Enter key is indistinguishable from a frozen computer.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY IT IS ONE LINE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `include login` means: use whatever this machine's ordinary login rules are.
#
# That single word is doing a lot of work, and all of it is work we do not want
# to do ourselves:
#
#   * The machine gains a fingerprint reader -> the lock screen gets it, because
#     the fingerprint step is added to the shared login rules.
#   * A password policy, a smart card, two-factor -> the same.
#   * Somebody changes their password -> nothing here needs to know.
#
# PAM's `include` works PER KIND OF RULE. This file only declares an `auth`
# rule, so only the `auth` half of /etc/pam.d/login is pulled in. The `account`,
# `password` and `session` halves — which set up SELinux contexts, the login
# audit id and a private /tmp — are deliberately NOT run, because a lock screen
# is not opening a new session. It is asking one question about somebody who is
# already logged in.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS IS EXACTLY WHAT EVERY OTHER LINUX LOCK SCREEN SHIPS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# We checked, inside the real Fedora packages. /etc/pam.d/swaylock,
# /etc/pam.d/hyprlock and /etc/pam.d/gtklock are each this same one line. So
# this file is not a clever arrangement of our own; it is the ordinary,
# well-trodden one, which for anything touching passwords is the point.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTHING HERE NEEDS SPECIAL POWERS, AND THAT IS THE GOOD PART
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shell runs as you, and so does the small process it forks to talk to PAM.
# Neither can read /etc/shadow, where passwords actually live — that file is
# root's. What happens instead is that PAM's `pam_unix` module hands the
# question to /usr/bin/unix_chkpwd, a tiny program that comes with Fedora's
# `pam` package, is allowed to read that file, and will ONLY EVER CHECK THE
# PASSWORD OF THE PERSON WHO RAN IT.
#
# So nothing AquariusOS ships is setuid, nothing of ours reads a password file,
# and the one thing that does is Fedora's, is three hundred lines long, and has
# been looked at by everybody.
#
# Plain-language write-up: the shell repository's docs/lock-screen.md, under
# "How the password is actually checked".
# =============================================================================

auth       include      login
